  "full_response": "Attention mechanisms are a fundamental component of transformer models, enabling them to effectively weigh the importance of different parts of the input data when making predictions. Here's an overview of how they work:\n\n### Overview of Attention Mechanisms\n\n1. **Purpose**: The core idea is to focus selectively on different parts of the input sequence to compute a representation of the output. This differs from traditional sequential models, which process data in a fixed order, making it harder to dynamically prioritize parts of the input.\n\n2. **Types of Attention**: There are various types of attention mechanisms, but the simplest and most widely used in transformers is the \"Scaled Dot-Product Attention.\"\n\n### Scaled Dot-Product Attention\n\nThe Scaled Dot-Product Attention can be broken down into a few key steps:\n\n1. **Inputs**: The function takes three vectors as input:\n   - **Queries (Q)**: These are vectors representing the element of the sequence you want to focus on.\n   - **Keys (K)**: These are vectors representing the elements you are comparing the queries against.\n   - **Values (V)**: These contain the actual information that will be aggregated into the output.\n\n2. **Compute Scores**: The attention score is calculated as the dot product between the query and all keys:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Score}(Q, K_i) = Q \\cdot K_i^T\n   \\]\n   This score measures the relevance or compatibility of the query with each key.\n\n3. **Scaling**: The scores are scaled down by the square root of the dimension of the keys (\\(d_k\\)):\n   \\[\n   \\text{Scaled Score}(Q, K_i) = \\frac{Q \\cdot K_i^T}{\\sqrt{d_k}}\n   \\]\n   This scaling prevents the softmax from having extremely small gradients during training, especially when \\(d_k\\) is large.\n\n4. **Softmax**: A softmax function is applied to the scaled scores to obtain weights that sum to one:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Attention}(Q, K, V) = \\text{softmax}\\left(\\frac{Q \\cdot K^T}{\\sqrt{d_k}}\\right) V\n   \\]\n   This process converts scores to probabilities, emphasizing certain keys over others.\n\n5. **Weighted Sum**: Finally, compute a weighted sum of the values, where the weights are the softmax probabilities calculated above:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Output} = \\sum_i \\text{Attention}(Q, K",
